      "id": "bbc-2026-09-19-bitter-row-over-diana-book-brings-back-unhappy-times-for-roy",
      "title": "Bitter row over Diana book brings back unhappy times for royals",
      "summary": "Many of the Royal Family's current troubles can be traced back to the aftermath of the then-Princess of Wales' death.",
      "body": "The disputes that followed Diana's death, within the family and between in-laws, are still being aired loudly in Earl Spencer's book - Swan Song: Diana, My Sister - and in the global news coverage it has triggered. It opens up old questions about King Charles's character, his treatment of the young princess and his relationship with the now Queen Camilla.On social media, Diana's fans are believing Earl Spencer's version of events, says Prof Pauline Maclaran of Royal Holloway, University of London. She says the royals remain \"highly vulnerable\" to anything that evokes the story of a \"beautiful princess whose life was cut short in such tragic and ambiguous circumstances\".Royal commentator Jennie Bond described it as a \"fantastically hostile, absolutely rude book... it's a very unpleasant portrayal of a man - but it's his memory of it, and who knows who's right?\"At issue is Earl Spencer's account of a conversation with the then Prince Charles after the death of Diana, with the prince accused of telling Earl Spencer: \"We'll forget her soon enough.\"He is accused of sounding \"giddily elated\" and like a \"lottery winner\" after the news of her death.Buckingham Palace hit back with a response, which although dressed in courtier-like politeness, was essentially saying the conversation had never happened like that.The King was said to be \"mindful that the pain of fraternal grief can cloud reason, affect judgment and colour memory in ways that others do not recognise\".Anyone who has lost someone knows how feelings can run high, people can hear different things about the same conversations - and families can fall out, or come together, in the wake of a death.Psychotherapist Nick Hatter says he doesn't have a view on which version of events is correct, but he says that during traumatic events memories can be \"distorted\". A phone call doesn't have any of the visual clues or body language to give a fuller picture, he adds.Hatter says there can be very different and unpredictable responses to grief, including a condition known as \"bereavement mania\" where someone can seem inappropriately hyper or even euphoric after the loss of a loved one.The first opinion poll on who to believe has been published by YouGov - and it suggests a very split view, with 31% believing Earl Spencer's allegations, 36% suspecting it's not true and 33% saying they don't know.Low thinks the book is unlikely to change many opinions, as \"people have settled into their camps about how they regard Charles, how they look back on the Diana years\".There could also be a feeling that it's \"tawdry\" to rake up these claims so many years later, he says.That's echoed by historian and author Sir Anthony Seldon who says Earl Spencer showed great courage at the time of Diana's funeral, but says his memoir is a \"sad and unnecessary publication\".Royal commentator Richard Palmer says the book threatens to take the royals back to the difficult days of 1997.\"This book and the run-up to next year's 30th anniversary of Diana's death threaten to undermine the Royal Household's three decades of work to rehabilitate Charles and Camilla's image,\" he says.Veteran broadcaster David Dimbleby, who led the BBC's coverage of Diana's funeral, said it seemed \"odd\" Earl Spencer would release the book now. \"Why 30 years later for god sake you should do it, well again you have to ask him,\"  he said, adding he wished the appetite for the \"cycle of gossip\" would decline and instead focus would shift to what kind of monarchy the country needs. Earl Spencer, in a statement ahead of the book's launch, said he had been \"inundated\" with requests for interviews about his sister, with the upcoming 30th anniversary prompting him to write down his own memories \"rather than having the discomfort of again reading the opinions of others, many of which are based on untruths that have become accepted over time\". He is fiercely defending the accuracy of his claims, in interviews over the weekend, including with the BBC's Laura Kuenssberg.In it Earl Spencer doubles down on his allegations about King Charles, saying: \"I can tell you here and now, those are exactly the words he said.\"I was hyper alert to what he was saying and those are precisely the words that he said and I promise that to you now. Genuinely, that is what he said. I don't hear him saying he didn't say that.\"In person, Earl Spencer is a very affable and charming figure, a writer who has had a first-hand view of some big royal moments and has the stories, sometimes scandalous, to show for it.What makes his claims more potentially damaging is that he isn't writing as a pundit or commentator, but as someone who was sitting around the table with his in-laws and his own sister. It's a close up, emotional, insider's view, not a long lens and a headline.If this book shows that the royals haven't escaped the legacy of Diana's death, then  it also shows that neither has he.You can watch Earl Spencer's interview with Laura Kuenssberg on Sunday from 16:00 BST on BBC iPlayer and on BBC One at 18:30.",
