    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-why-an-american-football-game-played-the-canadian-national-a",
      "title": "Why an American football game played the Canadian national anthem",
      "summary": "The field in Buffalo was decked out with the flags of both countries, as tensions simmer and a trade war escalates.",
      "body": "Many Americans at the game expressed their own frustrations with the escalating trade war, and the way it has divided the two neighbours. For Drew Grier, an American who lives eight minutes from the stadium, \"the anthem playing tonight is a core part of who we are as a border city\". At a Buffalo Sabres playoff ice hockey game in April, a microphone died while the Canadian anthem was being sung and the audience's voices filled in.\"You sang the Canadian anthem because it needed to happen,\" Grier said. Canadian Blake Parnham recalled that moment: \"I was sobbing watching the game at home because I felt so vindicated, that Buffalo is different.\"He couldn't make it to Buffalo for the Bills home opener, but he enjoyed it from his elaborately decorated backyard in Keswick, Ontario.Grier said that singing the Canadian anthem tonight is \"not that polarising of a thing\".\"If anyone is polarising it, it's coming from those outside here, because those of us here, we love Canada,\" he said.But some thought it was a step too far.\"The only acceptable reason to sing that anthem would be if they were playing a Canadian team, but they don't play any Canadian teams,\" posted one fan on social media.\"I don't think it's really necessary,\" said David Waring, who attended Thursday's game.In the end, it may all come down to simple economics. The Bills' new home, Highmark Stadium, cost $2.2bn to build. While the population of western New York is a little over 1.5 million, the nearby Toronto and Hamilton greater metro areas is home to more than 8 million. Simply put, the team needs Canadian fans.Gavin Kemp, who has run the NFL-Canada office for eight years, told the BBC that Canada's relationship to the NFL was \"extremely important\".Asked why the Bills chose this year to play the Canadian anthem, Kemp said the team was \"leaning into one of their biggest parts of their market\".Canada boasts nearly 15 million NFL fans – about one-third of the country's population, and roughly 10-15% of Bills season ticket holders are Canadian, according to the team.\"Sports really transcends all of this,\" Kemp said. \"All this politics is kind of secondary to the fact that your team is doing fantastic right now.\"Even American fans who traditionally cheer for other teams found themselves siding with the Bills.Carol Breed, who backs the Detroit Lions, considered travelling to Highmark Stadium from her home in Battle Creek, Michigan, after she heard they would play the Canadian national anthem.\"I love our country's relationship with Canada,\" the 74-year-old told the BBC, adding that in Michigan the country is viewed more as a \"relative\".\"I just hate what Trump has done to the partnership.\"She wasn't able to watch the game in person, but has found other ways to scratch her O'Canada itch.A couple of weeks ago, Breed embarked on the two-hour drive across the border, on the recently opened Gordie Howe International Bridge, which has also stoked divisions between the two nations of late.\"We sang the US anthem until we reached halfway and then started singing the Canadian anthem,\" she said.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T15:14:40.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/088b/live/dc7b2e60-b372-11f1-9023-9990160baacc.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cj5ydk2y0gg2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-18-brendan-carr-s-fcc-is-more-worried-about-who-the-view-interv",
      "title": "Brendan Carr’s FCC is more worried about who The View interviews than foreign governments owning Paramount",
      "summary": "The FCC has announced it's waiving its rules limiting foreign equity ownership to 25 percent in the Paramount-Warner Bros. case and will allow three sovereign wealth funds run by the governments of Saudi Arabia, Qatar, and Abu Dhabi to own 49.5 percent of the company. \nDuring Brendan Carr's tenure, the FCC has repeatedly threatened ABC, tried to block stations from airing interviews with Democrats",
      "body": "The FCC has announced it’s waiving its rules limiting foreign equity ownership to 25 percent in the Paramount-Warner Bros. case and will allow three sovereign wealth funds run by the governments of Saudi Arabia, Qatar, and Abu Dhabi to own 49.5 percent of the company.\nDuring Brendan Carr’s tenure, the FCC has repeatedly threatened ABC, tried to block stations from airing interviews with Democrats, censored late-night TV hosts, and tried to bully journalists. However, it seems to have no qualms with repressive foreign governments owning a significant stake in a giant American media conglomerate.\nIn its ruling, the FCC defended the decision, saying that, because the stocks being purchased did not have voting rights, they “will not be able to wield any influence, let alone control, over decisions involving the Licensees.”\nOf course, many, including the advocacy group Free Press and a number of Democrats, are not buying that. According to Variety, Free Press noted its opposition, saying that “control over for-profit, commercial domestic news media by any government is an extraordinary situation that would surely strike most Americans as unseemly, precisely because of the utility of the news media as a propaganda tool for those governments.”\nAnna Gomez, the FCC’s lone Democratic commissioner, posted on X, “The FCC just let some of the most repressive governments in the world indirectly control nearly all of a combined Paramount-Warner Bros. An investment this large in one of America’s biggest media companies doesn’t just buy equity, it secures influence over what gets said and made.“",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Policy",
        "Politics"
      ],
      "author": "Terrence O’Brien",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T15:12:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/04/gettyimages-2262365040.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/policy/997416/brendan-carr-fcc-foreign-governments-paramount",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-18-our-brain-evolved-from-two-primitive-nervous-systems-that-me",
      "title": "Our brain evolved from two primitive nervous systems that merged: Study",
      "summary": "https://archive.is/1CFX9\nhttps://med.stanford.edu/news/all-news/2026/09/two-separate-...\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49755533\nPoints: 43\n# Comments: 23",
      "body": "A 9.5-day-old mouse embryo. The front of the brain is blue and the back of the brain is red, which extends into the spinal cord\nOur brain may be a hybrid of two ancient nervous systems that were packaged together hundreds of millions of years ago. This is based on the finding that the front and back brain regions in people and several other species develop from two distinct cell types in embryos, instead of sharing the same developmental origin, as previously thought.\n“Our research suggests that evolution took two existing neural systems and pushed them together spatially,” says Kyle Loh at Stanford University. “Having the brain as one organ would probably be more efficient, but we rely on this primordial way to make the brain as two separate pieces.”\nLoh and his colleagues studied early stages of mouse embryo development and found its brain develops from two types of early progenitor cells, proliferative cells with a limited capacity of self-renewal. One type expresses a gene called OTX2 and turns into the neurons found in the front part of the brain, comprising the forebrain and midbrain. The other expresses a gene called GBX2 and becomes the neurons in the back part of the brain, the hindbrain.\nThe researchers then conducted experiments using human cells in a dish and found that the neurons of the hindbrain and those of the forebrain and midbrain also develop from different progenitor cells. “We’ve shown for the first time that the front of the brain arises from a totally different progenitor cell than the back of the brain,” says Loh.\nThis explains why it has been so hard to grow human hindbrain tissue in a lab. “It was actually a summer student’s failed experiment that got us into this,” says Loh. Researchers have typically tried making hindbrain neurons from the progenitor cells that are destined to become forebrain and midbrain neurons, which doesn't work, he says.\nArmed with this knowledge, the team was able to grow functional human hindbrain motor neurons in a dish for the first time by starting with the correct type of progenitor cell. &nbsp;\nThe hindbrain coordinates fundamental life-sustaining processes, such as breathing, sleeping, eating and the beating of the heart, whereas the forebrain is the centre of higher-level thought. Conditions like amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), the most common type of motor neuron disease, and spinal muscular atrophy cause speech and swallowing difficulties because they affect the hindbrain. Recently, scientists also discovered that GLP-1 drugs like Ozempic and Wegovy suppress appetite in mice by acting on the hindbrain.\nNow that it is easier to grow human hindbrain neurons in a dish, it should assist ALS and spinal muscular atrophy research, and allow us to investigate the precise mechanisms of how GLP-1 drugs work in people, says Loh.\nThe researchers also studied early-stage embryos of chickens, zebrafish and acorn worms, and found their nervous systems are similarly derived from two different types of progenitor cells. This suggests our shared two-origin brain system arose at least 550 million years ago.\nBut jellyfish, which we diverged from around 600 to 700 million years ago, have two separate nervous systems. These may have joined up in our distant ancestors because it improved their overall processing power, says Loh. “You get more efficient communication when things are closer together,” he says.\nHaving our brains develop along two separate pathways might also have facilitated the evolution of complex thought, says Loh. While the hindbrain was taking care of all the basic functions for keeping us alive, “evolution could play around with the forebrain, and make mistakes and give rise to all the fancy things like memory and creativity”, he says.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Brains",
        "human evolution"
      ],
      "author": "Jimmc414",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T15:12:07.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.newscientist.com/article/2589739-our-brain-evolved-from-two-primitive-nervous-systems-that-merged/#primaryimage",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.newscientist.com/article/2589739-our-brain-evolved-from-two-primitive-nervous-systems-that-merged/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-gabriel-15-in-barcelona-amid-speculation-over-future",
      "title": "Gabriel, 15, in Barcelona amid speculation over future",
      "summary": "Man Utd youngster JJ Gabriel travels to Barcelona as some of Europe's biggest clubs continue to vie for his signature.",
      "body": "Gabriel has not trained at United since scoring a hat-trick on his Uefa Youth League debut on 10 September. Tournament rules prevent him from playing for a side other than United in this season's competition.Sources close to Gabriel were furious the club did not register him for their Champions League opener with Sabah FK, which Michael Carrick's side won 4-0.The United manager was again asked about Gabriel on Friday at his news conference to preview Sunday's Premier League match against Fulham.\"He's a young boy, JJ, and I think it's important we don't play anything out here,\" he said. \"With the exposure and scrutiny and everything, you got to be careful and be sensible and think of him as much as anything.\"The situation will work itself out, whatever way it's going to work itself out. Making sure he's OK is the important thing.\"Gabriel scored in his first two Premier League 2 outings for United this season but will not be involved against Brentford at Leigh Sports Village on Friday.",
