      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-family-of-jagtar-singh-johal-say-scot-detained-in-india-has-",
      "title": "Family of Jagtar Singh Johal say Scot detained in India has been granted bail",
      "summary": "Th Sikh activist from Dumbarton was arrested in 2017 in India in connection with alleged terror offences.",
      "body": "Image source, Free JagtarImage caption, Campaigners say there is no credible evidence against Jagtar Singh JohalByKatie Hunter BBC ScotlandThe family of a Scottish Sikh activist who has been locked up in India for almost nine years without conviction have said he has been granted bail.Jagtar Singh Johal, from Dumbarton, was arrested in 2017 in connection with alleged terror offences.He was arrested for his alleged role in a series of targeted killings of religious and political figures.His family said they were still waiting for the details of what will happen next but are  \"relieved\" by the Indian court's decision.The 39-year-old was acquitted last year in a case in which he was accused of financially supporting terror group the Khalistan Liberation Force (KLF), which has carried out attacks in the Punjab region.But he still faces federal charges lodged by the Indian authorities.Johal's imprisonment was recognised by a UN panel as arbitrary detention in 2022 and he has claimed to have been tortured.Indian authorities have previously denied the torture claim and have repeatedly maintained due process is being followed.He was arrested in the Punjab region just weeks after his wedding in the country.His supporters say he was targeted for blogging about alleged human rights abuses against the Sikh community in India and was coerced into signing a false confession to participation in the killings.His brother Gurpreet Singh Johal has lobbied successive governments calling for his brother's release.\"It is a really important milestone for Jagtar's campaign,\" Gurpreet told the BBC's Radio Scotland Breakfast programme.\"It has been a long haul and an expensive rollercoaster but the community and the public have kept me going to campaign to bring Jagtar home.\"This will be a huge relief for him.\"Gurpreet said he did not know the exact bail conditions but expects money will have to be put down and Jagtar will have to stay in India or need permission to leave.He added that while it is a major step and the family are relieved Jagtar can be at liberty, more work will be required to bring his brother back to Dumbarton. \"It is a momentous moment for the family, his wife has been crying since this morning,\" Gurpreet added.\"Everyone was joyful, my kids went to school saying they cannot wait to see their uncle coming home.\"It is just a first step, but it is a step in the right direction.\"Image source, Gupreet Singh JohalImage caption, Jagtar Singh Johal (right) with his brother GurpreetGupreet previously urged the UK government to provide \"diplomatic representation\" to bring his activist brother home.He described his last meeting, with then-Foreign secretary Yvette Cooper in December, as \"disappointing\".At the time, a spokesperson for the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office said concerns about Johal's \"prolonged detention\" were raised with the Indian government at every appropriate opportunity.Johal was previously denied bail in 2024. According to Indian media, bail was granted by Delhi High Court.The conditions of the bail order remain unclear.Johal's family have insisted that no concrete evidence has been presented against him.They say the case was built on the testimony of unreliable witnesses, some of whom have given statements against him only for them to be later recanted in court.'Flawed proceedings'Johal is represented in the UK by the legal charities Redress and Reprieve, which have welcomed the judgement.Reprieve's deputy executive director Dan Dolan said the news was welcome but Johal still faces the death penalty for \"trumped up charges\".He said: \"We must now watch closely to ensure this order is actually implemented and not obstructed in any way.\"The conditions attached are likely to be onerous.\"Redress senior legal advisor Chris Esdaile said granting bail \"can't undo the intolerable suffering caused by nine years of arbitrary detention\".He added: \"The need for accountability for the allegations of torture and the deeply flawed proceedings against Jagtar remains urgent.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T08:45:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/791c/live/36d28420-f8e5-11ef-9e61-71ee71f26eb1.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwp845p6q2qqo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-tailgate-parties-marching-bands-and-ted-lasso-wembley-hosts-",
      "title": "Tailgate parties, marching bands... and Ted Lasso? Wembley hosts US college game",
      "summary": "Wembley will host its first American college football game on Saturday and one of Kansas' most famous fans could be among the 60,000 crowd.",
      "body": "Virtually all NFL players come via the US college system, and in many parts of America, college football is more popular than the NFL.College games often have bigger crowds - eight of the world's 10 biggest stadiums are for college football, all holding more than 100,000.The fans are also more passionate as support is often passed down from one generation to another, and multiple generations attend the same school. Kansas player Calvin Clements, for example, is a fifth-generation Jayhawk.Actor Paul Rudd is also a Jayhawk, while lifelong fan Jason Sudeikis grew up in Kansas and frequently weaves KU references into his work.\"They're very spirited, and you get the tribalism, so it's the closest thing we have to European football fanbases,\" Dubiski told BBC Sport.Both the NFL and college football have tailgate parties, where fans gather in the car park  and eat and drink for hours before the game.But college football fans chant during games, which feature marching bands and spirit squads - made up of cheerleaders, dancers and mascots.\"We want the game to showcase what we think is one of the greatest sports in America,\" said Yormark.\"There's something special about college football - the pageantry, the traditions, the fandom. It's dynamic - there's a lot of energy and passion. It truly is an experience.\"According to the NFL, 14 million people in the UK identify as fans of America's biggest sports league.UJC organisers have tried to tap into that fanbase and the UK infrastructure the NFL has built, but also appeal to younger fans.It  formed partnerships with the British American Football Association (Bafa), British Universities and Colleges Sport (BUCS), and the Jacksonville Jaguars, who host an annual NFL game at Wembley.About 60,000 tickets have been sold and 15,000 fans are travelling from the US, with student sections to help create the typical college gameday atmosphere.\"We're really pleased with that in year one,\" said Dubiski. \"We never expected to sell the stadium out, and 60,000 is higher than the average Big 12 attendance.\"Dubiski agreed an exclusive multi-year deal with Wembley and is already working on games in the next three years. He might expand to other European cities and bring basketball games too.Yormark believes a positive experience for fans and players would constitute success, plus an exciting game.Former NFL quarterback Robert Griffin III, now a college football analyst with Fox Sports, agrees that \"we've got to bring excitement\".\"The way to encourage fandom is to create moments, so I'm happy the two teams coming over know how to score points - and score them in bunches,\" he said.Griffin also believes college football could ultimately be more successful overseas than the NFL.\"The fandom around college football is better, it just is,\" he added. \"It's more similar to world football so there's a better opportunity to connect, also because there's more teams. In the NFL there's 32, but in college football there's 400 you can fall in love with.\"And if you want to be a real NFL fan, you have to be a college football fan because that's the feeder system into the league and you get to watch these guys develop.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T07:41:13.000Z",
