    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-17-mixed-feelings-from-consumers-as-electricity-discount-to-be-",
      "title": "Mixed feelings from consumers as electricity discount to be given to NI households",
      "summary": "The one-off £63 reduction in electricity bills will be applied from next month.",
      "body": "Image caption, Alison said she appreciates the discount but it won't make a big difference \"over the course of a year\"ByJohn CampbellEconomics and business editor and Holly FleckBBC News NIA one-off electricity bill reduction will \"make a difference\" to some while others believe it is \"nothing in comparison\" to price rises.Households across Northern Ireland will get the one-off £63 reduction in electricity bills from next month and it will be applied automatically without the need for an application.Speaking to BBC News NI in Belfast city centre, Alison said the discount \"wouldn't make a lot of difference over the course of a year\".She added that more could be done, but it is welcome at this time of year whenever it is coming up to Christmas.Image caption, James said the discount doesn't offset the price increasesJames said when you count up the bills every week for a year it is \"nothing in comparison\" to price rises.\"It's a wee bit of hypocrisy when they're giving you back the change basically out of what they're taking,\" he said.\"They're taking thousands off you in gas, electric, heating oil.\"He added that there has also been an increase in the cost of commuting, when fuel and parking is taken into consideration. \"It just doesn't make sense anymore. Everything is so expensive. So it will mean nothing really.\"How it worksThe reduction is the local outworking of two schemes which apply in Great Britain, which has a separately regulated electricity market.The schemes are being funded by the UK government.Bank payment customers will have their bills automatically reduced while pay-as-you-go customers will receive £63 in credit when they top up their meter.The only complication for pay-as-you-go customers is they should not buy more than £112 of credit if they want to get the discount all in one go.That is because meters are set up to take a maximum payment of £175. Top ups over this value means the £63 payment will be split over a subsequent top up.'It will make a difference'Image caption, Jane welcomed the discount and said it will make a difference Jane said the discount \"would make a lot of difference\" to her, as despite having a coal fire the coal is also expensive.She said she doesn't use electric blankets and electric fires, she puts on extra layers to stay warm, so the discount would be helpful.Jane added that she will spend the money on \"more luxurious food and keep myself healthy\".Image caption, Ed said it isn't an \"awful lot\" but he won't turn it downEd said the discount is \"not an awful lot to be honest but £63 is still £63\".\"I imagine it will mean more to other people but I won't turn it down or send it back, every bit counts in this current climate,\" he added.Discount will 'provide some help for families'Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Economy Minister Dr Caoimhe Archibald said the money would help familiesThe Economy Minister Dr Caoimhe Archibald said the money would \"provide some help for families as winter kicks in and energy bills rise\".She said the money would \"provide some help for families as winter kicks in and energy bills rise\".Her department agreed the NI scheme with the government and will deliver it to consumers.Heating oil voucherLast week applications opened for a separate means-tested scheme to provide a £100 voucher to some heating oil users. It is jointly funded by Stormont and the UK government.The electricity scheme reflects two policies which apply in GB: a temporary cut in VAT and the removal of some consumer levies.The UK government decided not to apply the VAT cut in NI as it would have required agreement with the EU which it thought would lead to delay.There are due to be similar bill reductions in the next two years but the size of those will depend on whether the government continues with the VAT cut which was introduced as a temporary measure.It comes as energy prices continue to rise, driven by the US conflict with Iran.Data from the NI consumer council suggests 500 litres of home heating oil currently costs around £560, more than twice the price compared to this time last year.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-17T17:00:01.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3584/live/1e78a930-b2a8-11f1-819c-176371125270.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cjvgy5yqvpy2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-17-warm-words-on-canada-s-eu-associate-membership-but-no-guaran",
      "title": "Warm words on Canada's EU 'associate membership' but no guarantees",
      "summary": "There is no certainty that the idea will ever get off the ground, let alone become a blueprint for other countries, writes the BBC's Katya Adler.",
      "body": "Is Canada about to join the EU? Headlines across Europe indicate a lot of confusion right now.The EU constitution rules that possibility out as Canada is geographically outside Europe.But in a speech on Thursday to the European Parliament, Canada's Prime Minister Mark Carney said: \"Canada and Europe should secure our strategic autonomy through deep cooperation in the full range of strategic capabilities.\"Mark Carney was responding – extremely warmly – to an invitation by European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen for Canada to become the EU's first associate member.The EU and Canada already have a far-reaching trade deal (Ceta) in place but Carney insisted additional cooperation on energy and economic security, on critical and raw materials, education, tech and AI innovation, and defence would help safeguard all countries involved.Canada and the EU feel increasingly squeezed by the superpowers USA on the one side and China on the other – and by tech giants, too, that seem to operate supranationally.The idea of associate EU membership, Carney emphasised, would not be to form a third superpower or to create a bloc against anyone else.\"We do not pursue zero sum deals,\" he said. Instead he suggested like-minded nations should club together to create a new form of multilateral global governance in an increasingly turbulent world where – in the minds of some others - might is right.Only by countries joining forces and shunning isolationism, Carney suggested, could they close key capability gaps, build strength and ensure national sovereignty.This last may have been a nod to upcoming elections across Europe and the continent's increasingly powerful nationalist politicians. Their slogans – whether \"Italy First\" or \"France for the French\" or \"Make Poland Great Again\" – echo Donald Trump's call to Make America Great Again.Carney's words were hopeful and warm; the applause in the European Parliament rapturous and apparently heartfelt.Not so across the Atlantic. On Wednesday, Donald Trump dismissed the EU associate membership proposal out of hand, threatened to impose \"very heavy tariffs\" if the agreement came to fruition and warned he may view it as a \"hostile act\".But the US president is the unintentional matchmaker for this EU-Canada love-in.Traditionally, America is trade partner and defence ally number one for Canada and the EU. But Ottawa is currently embroiled in what it calls an escalating trade \"war\" with the White House. Both Canada and the EU have been stung by what they view as Washington's weaponisation of trade tariffs. They also fear they can no longer fully rely on the US to have their back in terms of security and defence.Canada is not alone among Europe's far-flung allies, now seeking to cosy closer to the EU, as they worry the US is becoming too unpredictable a partner. Japan and South Korea show an interest in sheltering under the EU umbrella too.For its part, the EU has recently hastened to secure a list of trade deals – with India, Indonesia and Japan for example – to intentionally diversify relationships.But there is no guarantee Canada's associate membership of the EU will ever get off the ground, never mind become a blueprint for other countries, like the UK wanting closer relations stopping short of full EU membership.Any deal would have to be approved by each one of the EU's 27 countries.No legal precedent exists, negotiations would be lengthy and potential conflicts of interest loom, over steel tariffs for example.A suggestion in May by Germany to make Ukraine – a country desperate for full membership of the European Union as soon as possible – an associate member was rejected by others in the EU.Some, including France's government, want to avoid EU non-members securing \"too good\" a deal. They fear eurosceptic forces at home and abroad could then encourage voters to clamour to leave the bloc.In the end, \"associate membership\" is just a label. Buffeted by adverse winds – from China, the US and Russia, the normally rigid rules-based EU is coming under serious pressure to show more flexibility in accommodating countries wanting to stand by its side.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-17T16:59:43.000Z",
