      "id": "theverge-2026-09-14-apple-home-s-new-security-camera-features-cost-as-much-as-60",
      "title": "Apple Home’s new security camera features cost as much as $60 a month",
      "summary": "New Apple Intelligence features for HomeKit cameras start at $10 a month and go up to $60. | Image: The Verge\t\n\nWith the public release of iOS 27 and tvOS 27, Apple Home is getting an injection of Apple Intelligence - but you'll have to pay more for it.\nApple Intelligence for Home brings AI-powered video summaries to HomeKit Secure Video, so you can get short text descriptions of who and what comp",
      "body": "With the public release of iOS 27 and tvOS 27, Apple Home is getting an injection of Apple Intelligence — but you’ll have to pay more for it.\nApple Intelligence for Home brings AI-powered video summaries to HomeKit Secure Video, so you can get short text descriptions of who and what compatible security cameras saw, along with the clip. It also adds a new video search feature and the ability to stitch footage from multiple cameras into one clip, making it easier to keep an eye on what’s happening around your home without scrolling through footage.\n[Image: AI-generated video summaries provide text descriptions of what a camera sees. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/IMG_7845-1.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nBut to get these features, you’ll need to pay $9.99 a month for the 2TB iCloud Plus plan (or $39.95 a month for an Apple One Premier subscription) — and that’s just for one camera. According to an Apple support page, to add Apple Intelligence to a second camera, you’ll have to pay $29.99 a month for the 6TB plan. For up to five cameras, it’s a whopping $59.99 a month for the 12TB plan. No word on whether you can add more than five cameras. (Recordings from HomeKit-connected cameras don’t count toward your storage quota.)\nThat’s way too expensive. While the improved video features are useful — I’ve been testing them in beta and have been impressed — they’re not good enough to justify that big a price jump.&nbsp;\nHomeKit Secure Video has always been behind a paywall, but it started at a very reasonable $1 a month. That’s for the 50GB iCloud Plus plan, which includes recorded footage from one camera, plus smart alerts and facial recognition. For more cameras, you bump up to 2TB / $9.99 a month (up to five), and 6TB / $29.99 a month or higher for unlimited cameras. But the cost for the Apple Intelligence features is a huge increase.&nbsp;\nI currently pay $40 a month for Apple One Premier, which includes 2TB of iCloud storage and gets me HomeKit Secure Video support for five cameras. With the new pricing tier, I keep those benefits and now get Apple Intelligence on one camera. But to get it on all five cameras, I’d need to pay an additional $60 a month. That’s $720 a year, for a total of $1,200.&nbsp;\nAmazon’s Ring and Google Nest charge around $20 a month for subscription tiers that include similar AI-powered features for their cameras, and both companies offer more features than Apple currently does. An iCloud Plus plan does include storage for photos, files, and device backups, and higher plans may get you higher limits for Siri AI and other Apple Intelligence features, but Apple hasn’t released any details about that.\nThe good news is that none of this changes what you get with the standard HomeKit Secure Video today, and the new software releases have added under-the-hood upgrades that improve speed and reliability, plus the service now supports 4K video resolution. The change only applies if you want the new Apple Intelligence video features. But while Apple Intelligence feels like a significant improvement for Apple Home, at these prices, the value just isn’t there.\nScreenshots by Jennifer Pattison Tuohy / The Verge",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Apple",
        "News",
        "Smart Home",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Jennifer Pattison Tuohy",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-14T22:28:52.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/06/268594_iOS_27_home_app_first_look_CVirginia2.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/994949/apple-intelligence-apple-home-icloud-plus-cost-subscription",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-14-who-has-made-troy-s-premier-league-team-of-the-week",
      "title": "Who has made Troy's Premier League team of the week?",
      "summary": "After every round of Premier League matches this season, Troy Deeney gives us his team of the week. Do you agree with his choices?",
      "body": "Who has made Troy's Premier League team of the week?After every round of Premier League matches this season, BBC football pundit Troy Deeney will give you his team and manager of the week.Here are this week's choices. Do you agree? Give us your thoughts using the comments form at the bottom of this page.Gianluigi Donnarumma (Manchester City): Not only is he one of the best goalkeepers in the world, but he is a big-match player. We've seen it year on year and he's just won Manchester City two extra points against Manchester United. He'll get them 10 points over the course of the season, he's that good. After the Community Shield, people were saying he wasn't good enough and they should have kept James Trafford, but he proved again why he's such a big-time goalkeeper.Daniel Munoz (Nottingham Forest): I thought he was a breath of fresh air in the win at Villa. No-one in that Forest team can do what he does. Pace, power, he can defend and offers you a lot going forward. I thought he was a steal. I can't believe Liverpool and Manchester United didn't go for him. Forest did unbelievable business getting him. Ezri Konsa (Arsenal): Had a tough old battle against Brian Brobbey as they beat Sunderland. Last season, he ended up bullying Gabriel for the equaliser and he wanted no part of it. Konsa said 'no problem' and stepped up. That penalty was never a penalty. Lewis Dunk (Brighton): Who knew the big 6ft 5in centre-half could put one in to the top bins, straight off the crossbar? Unbelievable. He goes in just for that strike against Coventry alone. Tarik Muharemovic (Leeds United): What a beast he is. I don't know where Leeds found him but he heads it, kicks it, runs through people. Didn't get the clean sheet against Newcastle but deserved it. Leif Davis (Ipswich Town): Very, very good. Very underrated. He delivered in a six-pointer because I think Palace are going to be down there, with a goal and an assist.Enzo Fernandez (Manchester City): He had his best game in the last two years against Manchester United. He actually did everything the team needed - ran with the ball, looked after it, bought fouls, tried his best to get others sent off. I thought he was a catalyst for the team. His best performance for City so far. Pascal Gross (Brighton): Goal and assists. The old head who they brought back steadies that ship - it's a really young team. He is even more important now with no Welbeck, no Milner. He is in and is a steady hand. Kevin Schade (Brentford): Two goals again at Bournemouth. I feel like he's the next one they'll sell. Pace, power, scores goals and works hard for the team. Mohamed Belloumi (Hull City): Was decent in the Championship but he's really taken to the Premier League. Looks like he's stepped up and thought he'll have a bit of this. Two goals away at Chelsea is huge. They probably felt like they should have won. Dominic Calvert-Lewin (Leeds United): Steady away, isn't he? Goes about his business. Got one goal against Newcastle, should have had two. Excellent as a forward. Leeds have done something to keep him fit and he's changed the mentality for sticking in there. Maybe it's the Elland Road factor. They won't allow for anyone to not give 100%. He stays in the game all the time. Enzo Maresca (Manchester City): An honourable mention for Mikel Arteta. Got the job done away again. We could have had Daniel Farke. We'll give it to Maresca. I'm not his biggest fan, but tactically he got it right at Old Trafford. He understood the moment and gave his team the best platform to do what they needed to win. Made a brave call in bringing Cherki off for Ndiaye. Do you agree with Troy's selections? Who would be in your Team of the Week? Have your say using the comments form below:More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-14T22:18:48.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/8410/live/8bad5110-b080-11f1-b1d1-571ed4d7ff2c.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cxr50zln2gqro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
