      "id": "bbc-2026-09-14-police-had-councillor-s-fingerprints-on-hate-mail-but-droppe",
      "title": "Police had councillor's fingerprints on hate mail - but dropped case",
      "summary": "Gerard Woodhouse's fingerprints were found on an offensive letter before he targeted another victim.",
      "body": "Police had fingerprint evidence linking a high-profile councillor to a poison pen letter up to 12 months before he went on to target another politician in a \"persistent and wicked\" hate mail campaign.Well-known Liverpool anti-poverty campaigner Gerard Woodhouse, 63, was given a community order last year for harassing ex-Lord Mayor Christine Banks – who he had known for 50 years – with anonymous letters and cards in the post between 2023 and 2025.But the BBC has learned that although an investigation that pre-dated his hate campaign against Labour colleague Banks yielded fingerprint evidence from a letter sent to a housing association boss, the inquiry was stood down because officers were \"unable to establish contact\" with the alleged victim after she left her job.Despite reopening the case earlier this year, police have now had to drop it because the time limit for prosecuting harassment allegations has expired.The alleged victim, Julie Fadden, 65, said if Woodhouse had been dealt with he might have been stopped before he began harassing Banks and other suspected victims.Merseyside Police did not respond to the BBC's questions about whether it had done enough to contact Fadden during the 2022 investigation.Merseyside Police reopened its investigation in February. Woodhouse was later arrested and questioned.But Fadden has since learned Merseyside Police will not be taking any further action.In a letter from a Merseyside Police detective constable, she was told: \"At the time of the incident a number of other offences [besides malicious communications] could have been considered and were maybe more suitable for this incident, including harassment.\"However, as harassment is a summary only offence then it carries a statute of limitations of six months so would have expired back in June 2022.\"This is why the matter was progressed with malicious communications as it is the best suited offence available, as it does not carry a statute of limitations.\"However, he said while the letters were \"offensive in nature\", the \"Police Decision Maker does not believe that they go beyond the limits of what is tolerable in society, aiming to shock, insult or degrade the victim\".Fadden said she was disappointed that the opportunity to prosecute Woodhouse had been missed.\"They said they didn't know how to contact me [when I left my job], but they had my phone number.\"When I went back to them, they told me they'd found a fingerprint on one of the letters.\"She added that she \"knew the police have got a hard job, and they've not got the resources they need\", but that \"if they had done what they needed to four years ago\", then Woodhouse's campaign against Banks may have been avoided.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
