    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-13-will-record-donations-to-reform-go-down-as-a-moment-of-polit",
      "title": "Will record donations to Reform go down as a moment of political change?",
      "summary": "Andy Burnham is likely to face fresh pressure to further change the rules on party donations, writes Jack Fenwick.",
      "body": "When news of Reform UK's two record-breaking £36m donations emerged this weekend, it felt as if Nigel Farage's inner circle was attempting a fightback.The last few months have proved difficult for the party, with a dip in the polls, a spike in infighting and a series of questions about money that simply won't go away.In contrast, the mood within the ranks of Labour and the Conservatives has been fairly chipper.Most Labour MPs feel Andy Burnham has got off to an impressive start as prime minister, while many Conservatives are feeling confident about Kemi Badenoch's recent reshuffle of her top team.But Reform has been quietly - and as of this weekend rather loudly - building a formidable war chest.Even before these two mega donations, Reform had raised £30.5m since the start of the year, more than double their nearest rivals.This extra £72m takes them into uncharted financial territory, which the party says will be used to hire the brightest policy minds, as well as up to 400 campaign managers in every target seat around the country.In a world of fractured politics, when seats can be won on increasingly smaller percentages of the vote, having a ground campaign that is effective at getting out the core voter base could become increasingly important.Some in Westminster have been less than convinced by Burnham's insistence that he won't call an early general election.It might be tempting for the prime minister to go to the polls before Farage has had time to splash the cash on a shiny new party machine.Some Labour MPs think that could factor into his thinking, but one Number 10 source insisted Reform's extra cash does not make an early election any more likely.Burnham is now likely to come under increasing pressure to further change the rules on party donations.Labour is already planning to introduce a cap on the amount of money a British citizen living overseas can donate.There has been much talk about whether that could impact the donations to Reform by cryptocurrency billionaires Christopher Harborne and Ben Delo, but when interviewed by the BBC, Angela Rayner refused to say whether the two would be caught up in the rule changes.The Liberal Democrats and Greens have also called for a cap for people based in Britain too, to prevent future mega donations.Burnham has indicated that he's open to having that conversation, but chose not to support an effort by some Labour MPs earlier this month to tighten the rules.Rayner confirmed this morning that it was still something being considered by ministers.The Electoral Commission is looking at what more should be done in this area and the calls from within Labour for further change will now grow stronger.Government sources insist this weekend's donations are not being viewed inside Downing Street as a game-changing moment in this debate.But people across the political spectrum are now asking whether this massive injection of money will be remembered as a key moment when politics in this country changed.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-13T17:51:19.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7114/live/73ceb2f0-af95-11f1-a540-61c3f7fc4e6c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr7dl593k8yo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-13-why-is-google-still-serving-dodgy-ads",
      "title": "Why is Google still serving dodgy ads?",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.atomic14.com/2026/09/13/why-is-google-still-serving-dodgy-ads\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49686445\nPoints: 845\n# Comments: 374",
      "body": "HELP SUPPORT MY WORK: If you're feeling flush then please stop by Patreon Or you can make a one off donation via ko-fi\nAI is really good at detecting deceptive adverts - why isn’t Google using it?\nA couple of months ago I noticed this advert popping up in the YouTube app. The main reason I noticed it is that I accidentally clicked it - I knew I was running out of space on my iPhone and a minor lapse of concentration caught me out.\nThese things can slip through even the best review processes, so I did my internet duty and reported the advert.\nThe response I got back was less than satisfactory:\nAgain, sometimes things slip through the net and people don’t always check things properly, so I reported it again. And got the same response.\nIn fact, multiple people have reported this advert and received the same:\nWe found that the ad doesn’t go against Google’s policies, which prohibit certain content and practices that we believe to be harmful to users and the overall online ecosystem.\nI’m a big fan of Hanlon’s razor.\nNever attribute to malice that which is adequately explained by stupidity\nHowever, if you were being uncharitable, you do have to question whether it’s in Google’s interest to remove adverts that get lots of clicks. After all, these adverts are probably performing well and bringing in lots of lovely 🤑.\nA simpler explanation is that the people reviewing adverts cannot properly review everything so things get missed.\nYou can’t help thinking that AI should come to the rescue here. Given Google has some pretty good models - surely it can put them to use?\nHere’s what Google’s Gemini has to say about the advert:\nMisrepresentation: Unreliable / Deceptive Claims\nThe Evidence: The banner embedded inside the ad explicitly mimics an iOS system alert modal (“iPhone Storage is Full”) complete with standard iOS typography, container styling, and mock system buttons (“Yes” / “No”). This trick visually deceives users into believing their device is throwing a native system-level alert, coercing them to click.\n2. Non-Functional / Deceptive UI Components\nThe Violation: Ads cannot feature non-functional or misleading interactive elements, such as fake dialog options, false close buttons, or radio choices that do not perform their implied system function.\nThe Evidence: The “Yes” and “No” options inside the graphic image are static visuals designed to capture clicks anywhere on the ad banner to trigger a store/landing page redirect, rather than acting as true system controls.\n3. Deceptive Fear-Based Tactics & Unverified Claims\nThe Violation: Advertising policies forbid using deceptive claims or scare tactics to induce panic and force immediate user action.\nThe Evidence: Stating “If you don’t free up space soon, some features may not work properly” inside a fake system popup fabricates a urgent state of failure on the user’s personal device, misleading them about their hardware status to drive app installs.\nDisapprove the ad creative immediately. Issue a policy violation warning/disapproval flag to the advertiser account under Misrepresentation (Misleading Ad Design). Repeated violations of this nature may result in full account suspension for deceptive practices.\nGoogle’s own model rejects the ad in seconds, yet Google’s review process approved it twice.\nCome on, meatbags - use some of the amazing AI tools you have access to.\nHELP SUPPORT MY WORK: If you're feeling flush then please stop by Patreon Or you can make a one off donation via ko-fi",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
