      "id": "bbc-2026-09-13-finally-got-our-points-hull-and-woad-shine-in-european-solhe",
      "title": "'Finally got our points' - Hull and Woad shine in European Solheim win",
      "summary": "Charley Hull gets the ball rolling as Europe's big guns finally came to the fore in Sunday's singles as they wrestled the Solheim Cup back from the United States with a 15½-12½ victory at Bernardus Golf Club in the Netherlands.",
      "body": "Charley Hull got the ball rolling as Europe's big names finally came to the fore in Sunday's singles as they wrested the Solheim Cup back from the USA with a 15-13 victory in the Netherlands.Hull, who had garnered just half a point across the first two days in an unprofitable partnership with fellow Englishwoman Lottie Woad, led Europe out again on Sunday and claimed a 3&2 win over American livewire Megan Khang.It delivered an immediate boost to Anna Nordqvist's team in the singles format that they have been unbeaten in since their last home defeat in 2015.Woad, who alongside Hull is ranked among the top 10 players in the world, also delivered, with her 2&1 win over fellow debutant Auston Kim sparking wild celebrations on the 17th green at Bernardus Golf Club.With Carlota Ciganda three holes up heading on to the 16th, it assured the hosts of reaching the 14½-point tally required to triumph in the biggest team competition in women's golf.\"It was the no-brainer to put Charley first,\" said Nordqvist.  \"We saw the stats and Charley and Lottie were actually top-four players that made the most birdies for the week. They just played really tough matches. \"People don't understand how good all these players are and how good the golf has been in the past three days.\"So Charley was a no-brainer, and then finishing strong with Carlota. As we know, she's done it in the past.\"Any question marks over Nordqvist's captaincy had revolved around repeatedly pairing her two star English players together over the first two days. And Hull was one of the first on the scene to congratulate Woad, with the quip, \"finally got our points didn't we Lottie\".Veteran Ciganda officially carrying her side over the winning line by beating Jennifer Kupcho could not have been more fitting as she became just the second player to secure the decisive point twice - having done so in 2023 - joining Catriona Matthew who did the same for Europe in both 2003 and 2013.On Saturday evening Kupcho had hushed the crowd and yelled \"vamos\" as she drained a 91-foot putt on the 17th and then knocked in a 30-footer on the 18th green to steal a point from the Spaniard in the fourballs. It felt like a moment that had turned the momentum of the match towards Angela Stanford's team but fewer then 24 hours later, Kupcho and her US team-mates had little to cheer with all the euphoria draped around those wearing blue and yellow.\"I was really happy to see that I was playing against Kupcho,\" said Ciganda. \"She's a fighter, I'm a fighter, and what she did was amazing on 17 and 18. \"But [on Sunday] I knew she had no chance. I was like, I want to kill you,\" joked the Spaniard.Having started the day level at 8-8, Europe's quartet of rookies deserve huge credit.Having impressed in the foursomes and fourballs, Spain's Julia Lopez Ramirez and France's Nastasia Nadaud once again demonstrated their suitability for the biggest of stages.Lopez Ramirez defeated Yealimi Noh 3&2, while Nadaud always looked in control against Andrea Lee to win by the same scoreline.Mimi Rhodes, who had partnered Nadaud to two victories, was only downed by a superb performance from world number one Nelly Korda, while Lauren Coughlin, Lindy Duncan, Alison Lee were the only others to put splashes of red on Sunday's scoreboard.Coughlin, who had already earned two-and-a-half points this week, ended Leona Maguire's unbeaten singles record. Duncan beat Maja Stark, while Alison Lee came from four holes down to complete the biggest singles comeback in the history of the Solheim Cup, with a final-hole victory over Esther Henseleit.But it was simply not enough for the visitors, whose wait for a first win on European soil since 2015 continues.\"The Europeans played great, and that's the way it goes,\" said Stanford. \"Their backs were against the wall, they had nothing to lose, and that's exactly what they did, they came out and played well.\"By the time Rose Zhang concluded her win on the 18th against Celine Boutier in the last match on the course, the European party was in full swing.As the US team returned to their team hotel on Saturday, a picture was posted on social media of them celebrating Kupcho's heroics on the 17th and 18th greens with a crate of beer.Those scenes will have certainly inspired Europe on Sunday and the home team managed the wet early conditions perfectly as the US toiled on the greens.\"When we showed up in July here, they were faster. And I don't think it had anything to do with the rain either,\" said Stanford when asked about the speed of the greens.\"It's my responsibility to get the team ready. This is the one thing I am kicking myself [over] is that I just didn't keep pushing. We got to figure out how to hit our putts a little bit harder, which is really difficult, to change the way you putt.\"So I'm a little frustrated with myself that I didn't drive that point home. We don't play on greens this slow usually,\"I don't think we played bad. I think we just didn't make enough putts. If you listen to everything I've ever said, Europeans always putt better. \"I don't know why. I still don't know why. I mean, I have a good idea, but I'm not going to say it right now. Apparently I didn't get to know the superintendent as well as I should have.\"",
