      "id": "bbc-2026-09-11-girls-given-legal-right-to-wear-trousers-in-northern-ireland",
      "title": "Girls given legal right to wear trousers in Northern Ireland schools",
      "summary": "Guidelines stipulate that school uniform policies for the 2027/28 school year onwards must include the option for girls to wear trousers.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, The guidelines stipulate that school uniform policies for the 2027/28 school year onwards must include the option for girls to wear trousers throughout the school year, including for PEByAdam MandevilleBBC News NIGirls in Northern Ireland are to be given the legal right to wear trousers in school if they wish to do so.  Revising guidelines, Education Minister Paul Givan said that, from the start of the 2027/2028 academic year, schools will be banned from prohibiting girls wearing trousers. There are already laws on school uniform in Northern Ireland, and Givan had previously said they give girls the legal authority to challenge their schools. Education committee members, however, had called on the minister to go further.Some assembly members have now accused the minister of making a \"U-turn\", with \"substantial sums of public money\" spent on drafting legislation \"to fix his error of judgement\".Guidelines 'legally binding'It comes after assembly members passed a new law last year, aimed at addressing the cost of school uniforms in Northern Ireland - with these updated guidelines on wearing trousers now legally binding under the School Uniforms Act.Speaking on BBC Radio Ulster's The Nolan Show, Givan said the change would come into immediate effect ahead of the next academic year. \"I don't need to go through the assembly to give effect to this,\" he said. \"Today, I am taking that decision, that will be formally communicated, schools will be hearing from me, and as of the next academic year...schools will be required that this has to be available if it is requested upon.\"The minister explained that this change will not apply during the 2026/27 academic year, in order to allow schools to change their uniform policies.Givan added that the change will also apply to physical education.'Embarassing U-turn'Image source, PA MediaImage caption, The education minister says the changes will come into effect next yearIn a statement, Sinn Féin MLA Cathy Mason described Givan's decision as a \"welcome but embarrassing U-turn\".\"This is an important win for girls and for all those who campaigned for this simple choice,\" she said.\"The minister had the opportunity to guarantee this right in his own school uniforms legislation but failed to do so. It should not have required further legislation from the Education Committee to force movement on something so basic.\"Mason added that girls' right to wear trousers \"should never have been controversial and it should not have taken this long\".\"We welcome that the minister has finally acted and will be watching closely to ensure he follows through and that girls are not let down.\"'Error of judgement'In a statement, Alliance's education spokesperson, and chair of the Education Committee, Nick Mathison also described the decision as a \"U-turn\".\"I want to thank all of the young campaigners who have been bravely raising their voices about this, and whose dedication will lead to seeing this wrong righted,\" he said.\"The committee will be doing their full due diligence to guarantee the wording used in this update will ensure all girls are granted the right to wear trousers as part of their school uniform.\"Mathison added: \"Minister Givan will have to explain why he took so long to move on this issue. Substantial sums of public money have been spent on drafting legislation to fix his error of judgement, and we have said all along that he could have avoided this with the stroke of a pen.\"A statement published on the Department of Education website said: \"The guidelines issued today stipulate that school uniform policies for the 2027/28 school year onwards must include the option for girls to wear trousers throughout the school year, including for PE and Games.\"The guidelines are legally binding under the School Uniforms Act which came into operation earlier this year.\"It added: \"The Act is designed to help reduce the cost of school uniforms and ensure that affordability, comfort, practicality and sustainability are at the centre of school uniform policies.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-11T09:50:52.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/4de1/live/102cf7d0-adbe-11f1-ac2c-11e32c6809db.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czxzw9117k5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-11-i-was-in-the-epicentre-i-m-so-lucky-to-have-lived",
      "title": "I was in the epicentre - I'm so lucky to have lived",
      "summary": "Paul Berriff says his life expectancy has been cut short because of being buried under rubble.",
      "body": "'I survived the epicentre of 9/11 - I'm so lucky'Image source, Paul BerriffImage caption, Paul Berriff went to film the aftermath of 9/11 but ended up buried under rubbleByAmy OakdenBBC Radio Tees and Seb CheerYorkshireWarning: This story contains graphic details of the aftermath from the 9/11 attacks.On 11 September 2001, photographer and documentary filmmaker Paul Berriff was living and working in New York City.He was caught up in the 9/11 attacks and still remembers the day in vivid detail.More recently, he has become one of thousands of survivors to be diagnosed with terminal illnesses connected with the long-term effects of that day.Now aged 80 and living near Northallerton in North Yorkshire, Berriff has recalled the events.\"Somebody came into the office and said a plane had hit the twin towers,\" he said.First thinking it was likely to be a light aircraft or helicopter, often seen flying around the landmark, he phoned his wife who was at their home in Lower Manhattan.She told him about \"fire engines and people running around\" and his \"news instinct kicked in\" and he travelled to the scene to document the events on camera.As Berriff, a sound recordist and assistant producer approached, they could see \"both towers were on fire\".\"We could see these black dots coming down from the side of each building and we wondered what it was,\" he said.\"As we got nearer, we could see it was people jumping, so we knew it was pretty serious.\"They headed to the base of the towers, where assistant fire commissioner Steve Gregory was sending firefighters in.Image source, Paul BerriffImage caption, Assistant fire commissioner Steve Gregory arrived at the base of the towers at the same time as Berriff\"Suddenly, there was this loud explosion to my left,\" Berriff said.\"The camera was on and I turned it round and looked up, just to see the top of the south tower peeling away, like a massive umbrella opening.\"Whilst firefighters shouted for everybody to run away, Berriff said he \"stayed there, looking through the small viewfinder on my camera to see 250,000 tonnes of metal and girders hurtling towards me\".After \"about two seconds\", he and sound recordist Lulu \"started running down the street\".\"The next thing I remember was my camera leaving my hand in slow-motion and everything goes black.\"He was unconscious for approximately 35 minutes, during which time the second tower also collapsed.After regaining consciousness, Berriff said his \"mouth was full of all this cement gunge stuff\".\"I could hardly breathe. I was hot, I was sweating, my trousers were burning. Total blackness, I've never seen blackness like it.\"After many minutes of crawling to try and escape the \"hell hole that I was in\", Berriff emerged from under the rubble.\"I could see everything's gone from colour to black and white,\" he said.\"It was like ash from a volcano.\"Figure caption, Watch: Berriff filmed the first tower collapsing in 2001After realising Lulu was no longer with him, Berriff returned to the area where he had been buried under the rubble.\"In the road about 100 yards ahead of me, there was bits of the tower embedded in the roadway,\" he said.\"The police later said I was probably the first alive person in the epicentre of it - it was just total devastation.\"He found Lulu's sound mixer and his camera, which he picked up before returning to his apartment block, where Lulu was waiting.\"She'd been grabbed by one of the fire officers and they'd run into an underground garage so she'd escaped all the major impact.\"It was at this point he realised he was injured, with blood covering his back.His wife accompanied him to hospital, where he was decontaminated as a precaution as emergency services were concerned that there \"might have been anthrax or other toxic chemicals that the terrorists may have put in all the planes\".\"So by now, they knew.\"Following treatment, they had no keys or other belongings so stayed at the home of a nurse, whose husband loaned Berriff clothes.\"I went into the sitting room and I'd still got the camera with me,\" he said.\"I pressed play and up came all this imagery of what had happened to me.\"Image source, Paul BerriffImage caption, Berriff visited Ground Zero as searches were still being carried outBerriff founded the Humber Rescue lifeboat charity in his back garden in Hessle, near Hull, in 1989.Six years later, he established a lifeboat station on the bank of the Humber.He was appointed OBE for his work in 2016 and volunteers now attend more than 100 callouts each year.'I'm so lucky'Twenty-five years on from 9/11, he is still feeling its impact.\"All of us that escaped that day thought we'd done well, then about three or four years ago, we started developing these illnesses,\" he said.\"All of us had ingested the cloud which contained body parts, glass, aviation fuel, you name it.\"We'd eaten all this, ingested it all.\"It had laid dormant for 20 years and we've all developed these fatal diseases.\"He was diagnosed three years ago with pulmonary fibrosis.\"My consultant said I've only got five years to live,\" he said.\"That was quite a shock actually, having survived it all that close and then 'bang' this happens.\"According to the 9/11 Memorial,, external tens of thousands of people have been diagnosed with illnesses linked to the attack.After 9/11, Berriff said he \"lives every day as full as I can\".\"The only thing that stays with me is that I actually survived it and I shouldn't have done,\" he said.\"I ran down the street with the sound recordist. I went straight down the street.\"Those that went to the left or right of us, they died.\"I'm so lucky.\"Get in touch",
